+# Consumer-Facing Automated Decision Duties in Colorado, Connecticut, and Texas
+
+## Executive summary
+
+As of August 15, 2026, Colorado’s 2024 high-risk-AI duties have a June 30, 2026 statutory start, but a federal order prevents attorney-general enforcement; a replacement begins January 1, 2027. Connecticut’s amended privacy law has operated since July 1, 2026 and lets consumers learn about, question, and obtain reasons for certain profiling outcomes. Texas relies mainly on a generally applicable privacy statute.
+
+Colorado links notice, reasons, correction, and appeal to a particular high-risk system and decision. Connecticut centers access to profiling information and outcome challenges. Texas centers a privacy notice, an opt-out, and appeal of a refused rights request—not of the automated decision. Each statute assigns enforcement to the attorney general rather than creating a private action, subject in Colorado to the federal stay.
+
+## Scope and legal status
+
+This brief covers generally applicable laws governing private entities, limited to transparency duties and consumer notice, explanation, correction, review, or appeal rights tied to automated consequential decisions. “Consequential” broadly covers access to housing, credit, education, employment, insurance, health care, or essential services, subject to each statute’s definition. Government-use requirements, local ordinances, unenacted bills, and sector-specific duties are excluded.
+
+Colorado has two layers. [SB 25B-004](https://leg.colorado.gov/laws/session-laws/SB25B-004/3/download) moved SB 24-205’s operative dates to June 30, 2026. SB 26-189 replaces that code part on January 1, 2027. An [April 27 federal minute order](https://ecf.cod.uscourts.gov/cgi-bin/DktRpt.pl?253513) bars attorney-general enforcement of either enactment for now without erasing the duties. Connecticut P.A. 25-113 amended section 42-518 effective July 1, 2026. Texas Chapter 541 took effect July 1, 2024. Texas HB 149 took effect January 1, 2026, but its private disclosure is health-care-specific and excluded here.
+
+## Comparison
+
+| Jurisdiction | Authority and status | Before or at use | After a result | Review or appeal |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Colorado | SB 24-205 duties operative June 30, 2026, but enforcement stayed; SB 26-189 replacement effective January 1, 2027 | Current law: notice before a high-risk system makes or substantially contributes to the decision; replacement permits a prominent point-of-interaction notice | Current law: reasons, system contribution, and data types and sources; replacement sets a 30-day deadline and system-detail request path | Current law: correction and appeal with human review if feasible; replacement: correction plus commercially reasonable human review and reconsideration |
+| Connecticut | Section 42-518 as amended by P.A. 25-113; effective July 1, 2026 | Privacy notice explains rights; consumer may confirm profiling and opt out of profiling for an automated legally significant decision | If feasible, consumer may question the result, obtain the reason, and review data; housing data may be corrected and the decision reevaluated | Controller must provide an appeal process for a refusal to honor a rights request, with a written answer within 60 days |
+| Texas | Chapter 541; effective July 1, 2024; HB 149 effective January 1, 2026 adds no in-scope general private notice | Privacy notice explains processing purposes, rights methods, profiling opt-out, and request appeal | No general explanation of an automated outcome; a refusal of a rights request requires justification and appeal instructions | Appeal concerns the refused privacy request, not the merits; written resolution due within 60 days, then an attorney-general complaint route |
+
+## State analysis
+
+### Colorado
+
+Under [SB 24-205](https://leg.colorado.gov/laws/session-laws/SB24-205/198/download), a high-risk system makes, or substantially contributes to, a consequential decision about education, employment, lending, government services, health care, housing, insurance, or legal services. A deployer is a business using such a system. Before the decision, it must disclose the system’s involvement and purpose, the decision’s nature, contact information, a plain-language description, and any applicable profiling opt-out.
+
+After an adverse decision, current law requires the principal reason or reasons, the degree and manner of the system’s contribution, and the types and sources of processed data. The consumer must have an opportunity to correct incorrect personal data and appeal; the appeal must permit human review if technically feasible, unless delay could harm the consumer. Notices must be direct, plain-language, accessible, and provided in the languages the deployer ordinarily uses.
+
+On January 1, 2027, [SB 26-189](https://leg.colorado.gov/laws/session-laws/4864/download) changes the trigger to ADMT that materially influences a consequential decision. It retains advance notice, allows point-of-interaction posting, and requires within 30 days after an adverse outcome a description of the decision and ADMT role plus a route to system, developer, and input details. On request, deployers must provide correction instructions and commercially reasonable meaningful human review and reconsideration. The attorney general enforces both versions as deceptive trade practices, but the federal stay currently applies; neither creates a new private action.
+
+### Connecticut
+
+Connecticut regulates profiling through its consumer privacy statute. Under the [2026 supplement to section 42-518](https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/sup/chap_743jj.htm), a consumer may confirm whether personal data supports profiling for a legally significant decision and may opt out of profiling in furtherance of any such automated decision. Controller thresholds and exemptions limit coverage; “consumer” generally excludes employment or commercial contexts.
+
+For a completed decision, the July 1, 2026 amendments add rights, if feasible, to question the result, be informed of the reason profiling produced it, and review the data. For housing, the consumer may correct incorrect data and have the decision reevaluated. Feasibility qualifies the rights, and correction-plus-reevaluation is domain-limited.
+
+The request process supplies a second appeal. A controller generally has 45 days to act; a refusal needs justification and appeal instructions. The conspicuous appeal process must resemble the original request process. Within 60 days the controller must provide a written result and reasons, and a denial must identify a way to contact the attorney general, who enforces the law.
+
+### Texas
+
+Texas Chapter 541 covers businesses processing Texans’ personal data, subject to entity, data, household-use, and small-business exclusions. Its privacy notice must disclose data categories and purposes and explain rights and appeals. Consumers may opt out of profiling that furthers a decision providing or denying listed consequential services, including lending, housing, education, employment, insurance, health care, criminal justice, or basic necessities.
+
+The law does not require notice that profiling affected a result, an explanation of that result, input correction for reconsideration, or human review. Its appeal protects the request process. A refusal generally needs a response within 45 days, justification, and instructions. An appeal receives a written decision and reasons within 60 days; another denial must point to the attorney general’s complaint mechanism. The [official overview](https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/consumer-protection/file-consumer-complaint/consumer-privacy-rights/texas-data-privacy-and-security-act) confirms the distinction between profiling opt-out and request appeal.
+
+HB 149 does not close the general outcome-notice gap. Its consumer disclosure applies to health care, while another disclosure applies to government agencies. Both are excluded as sector-specific or government-use rules. The Texas attorney general exclusively enforces Chapter 541 and HB 149; neither supplies a private action here.
+
+## Cross-state takeaways
+
+Timing, enforcement posture, and the object of review differ. Colorado’s current statute attaches to a particular use and adverse decision, but enforcement is stayed; its 2027 replacement adds a 30-day post-outcome deadline and a commercial-reasonableness qualifier. Connecticut lets consumers identify profiling, question the result, obtain a reason, and review inputs, with narrower reevaluation. Texas offers privacy transparency and an opt-out, while appeal tests only a controller’s handling of a statutory request. Thus appeal concerns the outcome in Colorado, the outcome plus request handling in Connecticut, and only request handling in Texas.
